Context: I am building a PowerShell script to automate the conversion of a messy, AI-generated Markdown file (Testing_Grok_file.md) into a PDF using Pandoc and MiKTeX (XeLaTeX) on Windows 11.

The Constraints:

Environment: The script must run on PowerShell 5.1 (Legacy). The user has PS 7.5 installed, but the Windows context menu "Run with PowerShell" forces version 5.1.

No Modern Syntax: You cannot use ScriptBlock Regex callbacks (e.g., [regex]::Replace($str, $pat, { param($m) ... })). This causes a "Parse Error" and immediate crash in PS 5.1. You must use foreach loops or simple string replacement.

Input File: The file is named Testing_Grok_file.md.

Formatting: The script must handle:

Unicode Normalization: The source uses "Mathematical Italic" Unicode (e.g., 𝑆, 𝑃, 𝑝) which must be converted to ASCII.

Chemical Isotopes: Syntax like ^{26}P or $^{26}$P must be converted to \ce{^{26}P} (requires mhchem package).

Line Wrapping: The source is one massive line (200k+ chars). It must be soft-wrapped to avoid LaTeX bufsize errors.

Broken Images: The source contains reference images ![Alt][1] and broken URLs. These crash Pandoc. They must be converted to Text Links ([Image: Alt]). Do not try to download them.

The History of Failures & Fixes:

Attempt 1 (Buffer Overflow): LaTeX crashed because the input was one single line. Fix: We added a regex to insert newlines every 80 characters.

Attempt 2 (Immediate Exit): The script used modern PowerShell syntax. Fix: We switched to strict PS 2.0/5.1 syntax (no callbacks, Trap block for error catching).

Attempt 3 (Unicode Chaos): Chemical regex failed because "P" was actually Unicode U+1D443. Fix: We added a map to replace specific Unicode ranges with ASCII chars.

Attempt 4 (Math Mode Errors):

Error: ! Missing $ inserted. LaTeX found symbols like \gamma or \cdot in plain text.

Error: ! Display math should end with $$. This happened when we tried to wrap symbols in $, but accidentally wrapped symbols inside existing $$ blocks (creating illegal double-math mode).

